nched forth into a tirade very different from his platform utterances. He spoke with extreme contempt of the dense stupidity exhibited on all occasions by the working classes.", "reference": "1918, W[illiam] B[abington] Maxwell, chapter XIII, in The Mirror and the Lamp, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": ["blag"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To deprive (somebody) of something by imposition.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "How came such multitudes of our nation […] to be sponged of their plate and their money?", "reference": "1692–1717, Robert South, Twelve Sermons Preached upon Several Occasions, volume (please specify |volume=I to VI), London:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To clean, soak up, or dab with a sponge.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Before stepping into the bath the head should be wet with cold water, and in the bath the pit of the stomach should first be sponged.", "reference": "1876, Eliza Bisbee Duffey, Our Behaviour: A Manual of Etiquette and Dress of the Best American Society, Philadelphia:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To suck in, or imbibe, like a sponge.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To wipe out with a sponge, as letters or writing; to efface; to destroy all trace of.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Lett the eyes which have looked on Idols, sponge out their unlawfull acts", "reference": "[1594], Richard Hooker, edited by J[ohn] S[penser], Of the Lawes of Ecclesiastical Politie, […], London: […] Iohn Windet, […], →OCLC, (please specify the page):"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, baking) To be converted, as dough, into a light, spongy mass by the agency of yeast or leaven.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(marine biology, of dolphins) To use a piece of wild sponge as a tool when foraging for food.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Why do dolphins sponge instead of foraging in a more \"normal\" way?", "reference": "2013, Pamela S. Turner, The Dolphins of Shark Bay, page 22:"}, {"text": "Sponging is worth thinking about in some detail because it illustrates many of the challenges and sources of controversy in studying social learning and culture in wild cetaceans.", "reference": "2015, Hal Whitehead, Luke Rendell, The Cultural Lives of Whales and, page 109:"}, {"text": "Moreover, the females that do it seem to \"sponge\" relentlessly throughout the day, hunting more than other females and more than male spongers too.", "reference": "2017, Janet Mann, Deep Thinkers: Inside the Minds of Whales, Dolphins, and Porpoises, page 153:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sponge", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}